# Spin Up a MarkdownLLM Domain

This session runs in an ephemeral container with no memory of prior sessions.
This skill rebuilds the working environment from git and fixes the operating
rules for the session. It is the Cowork adapter for MarkdownLLM (framework
v{framework_version}, mechanism {bundle_hash}): the bash layer only assembles;
every duty after the framework clone runs through the framework's own tested
floor (`mdllm assemble`), which emits each domain's Tier-0 contract INTO this
transcript rather than instructing you to go read it. The framework checkout is
the exact `FRAMEWORK_COMMIT` embedded at bundle build; it never executes a
later default-branch revision by surprise.

## Step 0 — Load the operating contract

Read `references/SESSION.md` before anything else. It defines the
non-negotiable rules: publication discipline, credential handling, commit
attribution, floor discipline, and the controls table saying which
guarantees are real in this harness.

## Step 1 — Get the access token

Ask the user to paste their GitHub fine-grained personal access token
(Contents: read and write on the repos in `references/config.env`). Do not
proceed without it.

Credential handling is strict: never write it to a file, never echo it,
never place it in a URL or `.git/config`. It travels only as a
process-scoped environment variable into commands that supply it through a
command-scoped header and redact it from output. If the user has no token
ready, ask them to create a short-expiry fine-grained PAT and paste it.

## Step 2 — Assemble

Run the bundled bootstrap from THIS skill's directory:

```bash
bash "<this-skill-dir>/bootstrap.sh" "<PAT>"
```

**Domain selection.** If the user named specific domain(s), pass the terms
as extra arguments — they match case-insensitively against each configured
`owner/repo` or repo name, so "qms" selects a repo with qms in its name:

```bash
bash "<this-skill-dir>/bootstrap.sh" "<PAT>" qms engineering
```

If a filter matches nothing, the output lists the configured domains —
relay them and ask which. If the bootstrap reports **BUNDLE MISMATCH**, tell
the user plainly: the assembly still ran on the pinned framework commit's
floor, and rebuilding the plugin from a clean, remotely available framework
commit restores the binding. This is not a latest-release signal.

## Step 3 — Read what arrived (the contract is already in context)

The assembly output is not a log to skim — it contains, per domain:

- **The emitted Tier-0 contract** — the operative kernel and the domain's
  `AGENTS.md`, in full, plus a reading list derived from the filesystem at
  emission time. **This is in your context now. Act under it.** Do not
  summarise it away; do not re-read what was emitted; DO read the listed
  domain skills at the moments the contract names — the write skill before
  ANY write.
- **The full trigger evaluation** — including the triggers the floor
  cannot evaluate. Judging those is yours; a relationship trigger nobody
  judges is an alarm nobody hears. Surface each verdict.
- **The imports COVERAGE line** — `0 stale` over unpinned imports asserts
  nothing; could-not-check and checked look identical in a summary, so
  relay the coverage number, not just the verdict.
- **The BRANCH MAP** — the ONLY branch names that may be published to,
  each read from its remote's own HEAD. Relay it to the user, one line per
  domain, so a wrong name is caught by them now rather than discovered
  later. A MISMATCH row means: do not publish until resolved.
- **The HANDOFF** — which controls ran mechanically and which remain
  yours with no backstop. If you are about to skip one of the "yours"
  items, say so to the user in plain words.

Then orient: relay the per-domain orientation in plain language, leading
with anything time-sensitive (deadlines and fired triggers), then open
conflicts, then open loops and version state.

## Step 4 — Work, then close every authorised commit

Do the work the user asks for, under each domain's own contract. The floor
hook validates every commit; never bypass it with `--no-verify`.

Publication is default-deny. Before the first write in each domain, inspect
its entry contract. Only literal `git.autopush: true` grants standing
publication authority; false, absent, or malformed is off. The PAT and
bootstrap invocation do not grant future push authority. If authority is off,
tell the user the disposable-container risk and ask whether they want to make
that future-facing domain change; do not make it for them.

When literal true is present, expect `autopush: AUTH-FAILED` after a commit:
the credential is command-scoped and deliberately absent from git config.
Close each authorised commit explicitly:

```bash
GH_PAT="<PAT>" python3 tools/mdllm.py publish domains/<name>
```

`mdllm publish` reads the recorded default branch (never accepts a typed
one), refuses to publish from the wrong branch, refuses to create a remote
branch, pushes fast-forward only, and re-reads the remote tip to prove the
commit landed. A non-zero exit means STOP and read the message — never
route around it with `--force` or a different branch name. Confirm each
publication to the user naming the branch.

Do not batch authorised publications: the container is disposable, and a
committed-but-unpublished change is lost when the compute is reclaimed. A
subagent inherits neither authority nor branch context; only after the same
literal-true check, hand it the exact publish command — "push to the default
branch" is an invitation to guess.

## Step 5 — Wrap-up

Run the publication-debt check and relay it:

```bash
python3 tools/mdllm.py estate-sync . --status
```

On a healthy session it reports nothing unpublished. Then remind the user:
the token was pasted into this transcript, so the only real kill switch is
revoking the PAT on GitHub now that the session is done.
